 produced CRLF in `.history`. Since the prompt forbids hand-editing `.history`, let me first confirm whether this CRLF is new (from the CLI) or was already present, and whether `.gitattributes` governs this file.\n[tool Bash] {\"command\": \"cd \\\"C:/GitHub/Berreman\\\" && echo \\\"=== 0d byte positions in .history (offsets) ===\\\"; od -c \\\"specs/0042/.history\\\" | grep -n '\\\\\\\\r' | head; echo \\\"=== count of \\...\nLet me count the actual CR bytes precisely with Python — the `od` grep is ambiguous.\n[tool Bash] {\"command\": \"cd \\\"C:/GitHub/Berreman\\\" && python - <<'PY'\\ndata=open(r\\\"C:\\\\GitHub\\\\Berreman\\\\specs\\\\0042\\\\.history\\\",\\\"rb\\\").read()\\nprint(\\\"total bytes:\\\", len(data))\\nprint(\\...\nThe precise count confirms it: `.history` has exactly 2 CRLF line endings — and the pre-existing row 1 (259 bytes = 257 + `\\r\\n`, matching the original file size) was *already* CRLF. So CRLF is the format the project's own `history_cli` (the mandated safe writer) maintains for this log; I neither introduced a deviation nor may I hand-edit it. My authored deliverables — `.spec-md` and `.spec-jsonl` — are correctly LF-only.\n\nEverything is authored and validated. Here's the summary.\n\n## Spec 0042 authored — \"Reproduce report §7.2 (the Mueller inverse problem) as a UI-less F# test\"\n\nI read the preliminary spec (`004-…md`), the task/comment files, and verified every source pointer against the tree before citing it. Both spec files were empty; I authored them from scratch in the two-file model.\n\n**`.spec-md` (steering narrative only)** — binding constraints + Parts A–E, ending in `## References`. No per-step acceptance/files/tests sections. Key constraints pinned: reproduce §7.2 + §8 only (tweaks deferred); port all three reference stages and **re-derive** the calibration constants (rounded report values are cross-checks); many tiny pure functions with elevated types; CSV access **and** the numeric solve behind `*Proxy` records; pure-F# linear least squares only (no nonlinear/Wolfram/Python at test time); skip-not-fail when the sibling `OPM` data is absent. Plausible-expansion exclusions named explicitly.\n\n**`.spec-jsonl` (11 forward-only steps)** — validated: valid JSON per line, contiguous numbering, forward-only `depends_on`, lifecycle-clean, final `WIRE`:\n- **A (CRITICAL):** `Retardance` + `retarderMueller`; the column-major `kron4`/`vec`/`unvec`/`frobeniusDiff` core with round-trip + transpose-guard tests.\n- **B:** `ADD`/`IMPLEMENT` `<SVC:MuellerSolverProxy>` (MathNet SVD default in `OpticalConstructor.Domain`).\n- **C:** `ADD`/`IMPLEMENT` `<STORE:MuellerDataProxy>` (contract in Domain reusing `Experiments.DataFilePath`; pure parser + file-backed factory in `OpticalConstructor.Storage`).\n- **D:** label parse/family/kind, source/analyzer models, effective states, Stage-1 cosine calibration, per-family signal reduction (+90/−90 invariance guard).\n- **E:** `buildDesign`/`reconstruct`/`cascadeProduct`, then the `WIRE` step assembling the real proxies in `BerremanTests`, loading the `OPM` data by relative path (`[<SkippableFact>]`), and asserting the §7.2 matrices (~2e-3, rank 16) + §8 cascade metrics.\n\nAll pure numeric work lands in a new Domain module `MuellerReconstruction`; the tests live in `BerremanTests` (with explicit `ProjectReference`s to Domain and Storage per review Q2).\n\n**`.history`** — appended one `agent / spec / output` row via the mandated `history_cli` with the cycle's bookkeeping values (`--at 2026-07-16T00:37:45Z`, `--commit-ref none`).\n\nNo questions were needed — the preliminary spec and codebase resolved every design decision, so I took the most-defensible interpretation and wrote the spec.\nThe precise count confirms it: `.history` has exactly 2 CRLF line endings — and the pre-existing row 1 (259 bytes = 257 + `\\r\\n`, matching the original file size) was *already* CRLF. So CRLF is the format the project's own `history_cli` (the mandated safe writer) maint